Planning and Development Office
2101 O’Neil Avenue, Suite 202, Cheyenne WY 82001
(Phone) 307-637-6282
(Fax) 307-637-6366

MEMO
To:
Honorable Mayor Patrick Collins and Members of the City Council
From:
Charles Bloom, AICP, Planning and Development Department
Subject: Urban Renewal Development Agreement for a development in the West Downtown Plan
and Project urban renewal area
Date:
October 14, 2024

Background:

The item for consideration is a development agreement to award tax increment financing (TIF) to a
developer within the West Downtown Plan and Project area. This is a three-party agreement between
the City, developer/owner, and their lender. At this time the lender has not been finalized and the
lender’s information will be completed when selected. The property is presently under contract and the
developer.

The proposed Governing Body review schedule is as follows:
•
Introduction

October 14, 2024
•
Public Services Committee
October 22, 2024
•
Final consideration:

October 28, 2024

The developer, Stencil Group, intends to construct a 101-unit five-story multifamily structure at the
southwest corner of 19th Street and O’Neil Avenue. The Andiamo Subdivision plat was approved at the
September 23, 2024, meeting of the Governing Body to allow creation of a single 1.05-acre lot for this
development. This plat must be recorded prior to the execution of this development agreement.

The Urban Renewal Authority Board approved this application at their September 5, 2024, meeting.
The September 5, 2024, URA staff report, third party TIF application review, and July 3, 2024, URA staff
report are attached and provide additional project information.

TIF Eligible Costs:

The applicant has proposed that a variety of costs be eligible for TIF reimbursement which are found on
Exhibit B f the development agreement. Staff and the developer have had discussions regarding eligible
costs and have not come to an agreement on what can/should be eligible.  Specifically, staff has
requested a further breakdown of costs related to each specified category and asked that aid for land
acquisition be removed. The developer has communicated to staff that Exhibit B will be revised to
provide further detail and a substitute exhibit will be offered at the Public Services Committee meeting;

  Planning and Development Department
2101 O’Neil Avenue, Suite 202, Cheyenne WY 82001
(Phone) 307-637-6282 (Fax) 307-637-6366
```

### Page 2 of 81

```text
however, they have indicated they would like to make their case for land acquisition assistance directly
to the Governing Body.

In review of eligible costs, staff looks for consistency with Wyo. Stat. § 15-9-113 and the Wyoming
Constitution (Specifically, Article 16, Section 6 (a) (i) and Article 3, Section 36). The biggest concern is
regarding how the application relates to the State Constitution. Relevant sections of the State
Constitution are as follows:
•
Article 16, Section 6, Loan of credit; donations prohibited; works of internal improvement.
“Neither the state nor any county, city, township, town, school district, or any other political
subdivision, shall . . . loan or give its credit or make donations to or in aid of any individual,
association or corporation, except for necessary support of the poor . . .”
•
Article 3, Section 36, Prohibited appropriations. “No appropriation shall be made for charitable,
industrial, educational or benevolent purposes to any person, corporation or community not
under the absolute control of the state, nor to any denominational or sectarian institution or
association.”

The above-mentioned sections of the State Constitution prohibit a political subdivision from loaning or
gifting funding to a private individual, corporation, or similar entity unless they serve a public purpose or
provide necessary support of the poor. Cheyenne has been careful with the programs within the Urban
Renewal Statutes (Urban Renewal and Downtown Development Authority) to ensure that all funds
passing through the programs have a public purpose. Staff has taken the stance that use of public funds
is appropriate for improvements within the right-of-way, and improvements on private property that
have a direct relationship to the public realm such as publicly accessible plazas/arcades and façade
improvements, or areas of private property publicly controlled by easement or other instrument.

Staff believes that most of the costs proposed are eligible and will meet the intent of Wyoming’s urban
renewal statutes and the State Constitution upon further clarification; however, staff expresses concern
with assisting with land acquisition and improvements that could be considered not of public benefit.

Attachments:
1. Urban Renewal Development Agreement
2. September 5, 2024, URA Staff Report; Third Party TIF Application Review; and July 3, 2024, URA
Staff Report (60-pages)
```

